      "headline": "The Magic Pill Fallacy",
      "keyInsight": "Imagine your car dashboard lights up with a flashing red oil warning. Instead of pulling over to fill the tank, a mechanic pops the hood and snips the wire. Presto! The warning light turns off. You drive away whistling, thinking the problem is solved. That is exactly how we treat sadness today. For decades, doctors handed out pills like magic wands. They told us our brain chemistry was simply out of tune. But a blinking light is not an engine malfunction. It is a vital alert system. When your life is running on empty, your sadness is not a random glitch. It is your emotional dashboard begging for genuine oil and maintenance. Muting the alert does not fix the road you are traveling on.",
      "content": "For years, Johann Hari believed his sadness was just broken wiring inside his skull. His doctor gave him a small white pill and made a grand promise. It was presented like a magic wand that would sweep away the gloom. Take this pill every morning, the doctor said, and your brain chemicals will snap back into place. For a short while, the trick seemed to work. The dark clouds parted, and everyday life felt lighter.\n\nThen the fog rolled back in. The familiar ache returned, heavier than before. When Johann went back to the clinic, the answer was always the same: double the dose. Up and up the milligrams went, like turning up the volume on a crackling radio to drown out static. The pill was supposed to fix a broken machine. Yet the quiet emptiness never truly packed its bags and left.\n\nEventually, Johann stopped staring at the pill bottle and began asking bigger questions. What if the sadness was not a broken part at all? Think of a smoke detector chirping loudly in your kitchen. If burnt toast fills the room, you do not smash the alarm with a broomstick. You open the window and clear the smoke. The alarm is working perfectly. Our low moods might just be alarms doing their natural job.",
