      "headline": "The Chemical Myth",
      "keyInsight": "We were sold a tidy fairy tale about brain chemicals. The story claimed happy people had plenty of serotonin, while depressed people ran dry. It sounded clean and tidy, like testing a car battery with jumper cables. Yet real research tells a wildly different story. Scientists discovered that boosting serotonin instantly does not cure sadness overnight. In fact, many major studies show pills work mostly through the sheer power of hope and expectation. Placebos do almost all the heavy lifting. We turned a deep human signal into an imaginary plumbing leak. Chemical imbalances did not create our despair. Living isolated lives in concrete boxes did.",
      "content": "Walk into any pharmacy and you will hear the legend of the chemical balance. It is a story told with neat diagrams of brain cells shaking hands. In this story, your brain is like a swimming pool. If the chlorine level drops, the water turns cloudy and green. Add a scoop of powder, and the water sparkles again. It makes emotional pain sound tidy, simple, and mechanical.\n\nYet when independent scientists pulled open the file cabinets, the math fell apart. Drug trials revealed a startling truth hidden in plain sight. Sugar pills produced nearly the exact same mood lift as brand-name drugs in average cases. Believing that help had arrived did most of the work. Even stranger, medications alter brain chemistry within hours, but patients only report feeling better weeks later. The swimming pool analogy was fundamentally broken.\n\nImagine sitting in a freezing house during a winter storm. You can take a drug that numbs your skin so you stop shivering. You might feel a little warmer for an hour. But your windows are still cracked open, and snow is still blowing through the front door. Low serotonin is not the storm outside. It is just the chill you feel when the walls come down.",
