      "headline": "The Grief Exception",
      "keyInsight": "For decades, psychiatry had a curious loophole written into its rulebook. If your spouse died, you were allowed to weep, stay in bed, and feel crushed for an entire year. Doctors called it natural grief, not clinical depression. But if you felt identical agony because you lost your job, your savings, or your sense of purpose? That was labeled an internal sickness. Then, the experts took an even stranger turn. They shrank the allowed mourning window down to just two short weeks. Cry on day fourteen, and you are normal. Cry on day fifteen, and you have a brain defect. It is total madness. Grief proves our pain makes sense. When life suffers a devastating blow, your heart is meant to ache.",
      "content": "Imagine you are tending a lush tomato garden in your backyard. A freak hailstorm hits overnight and flattens every vine to mud. The next morning, you sit on the porch feeling numb and heartbroken. Nobody would tell you that your brain cells broke during the night. Your garden is gone. You are reacting to real wreckage with honest tears.\n\nFor a long time, doctors acknowledged this obvious reality with something called the grief clause. If someone you loved passed away, you were allowed to be deeply sad without getting a disease label slapped on your chart. Everyone understood that a shattered heart takes time to mend. But if your life fell apart from bankruptcy or bitter divorce, you were told your serotonin was defective. The double standard was absurd.\n\nEventually, medical committees decided even mourning a spouse had an expiration date. They shrunk the grief window from months down to a couple of weeks. If you were still weeping on day fifteen, you were handed a prescription. This reveals the big flaw in how we think about mental health. We treat normal human responses to painful events as medical bugs. Pain is not a disease; it is the natural cost of loving and losing things that matter.",
