      "chapterNumber": 4,
      "headline": "The Mystery Signal in the Engine",
      "keyInsight": "Imagine driving your car down a quiet highway when a bright red warning light flashes on your dashboard. You do not grab a roll of duct tape, cover the glowing bulb, and congratulate yourself on fixing the car. That would be absurd. Yet for decades, this is how we treated emotional pain. We viewed deep sadness as a broken wire inside our skulls. Scientist George Brown decided to check the actual engine instead. He interviewed hundreds of ordinary women living ordinary lives. The pattern hit him like a lightning bolt. Women drowning in grief were not suffering from random brain glitches. Almost all of them had suffered massive, crushing blows in real life. They faced eviction, terrible relationships, or sudden job loss. Depression was never a malfunction of the brain machine. It was a completely logical alarm ringing because the house was on fire. Treating the alarm while ignoring the blaze misses the entire point.",
      "content": "Imagine you plant a tomato seed in a dark basement closet. You give it pure sand instead of rich soil. You never water it, and you keep the lights turned off. A few weeks later, the sprout looks yellow, limp, and withered. Would you stare at that dying seedling and conclude that it has a broken internal genetic switch? Of course not. You would look at the cold basement, the dry sand, and the missing sunlight. You would know instantly that the environment is starving the plant.\n\nFor a long time, standard medicine treated sad human beings like mysterious alien machines. If you felt hopeless, doctors assumed a chemical cog slipped out of place inside your brain. Then a quiet British researcher named George Brown asked an obvious question that everyone missed. What is actually happening in these people's daily lives? He spent months sitting in living rooms, listening to hundreds of women talk about their daily struggles, their marriages, and their unpaid bills.\n\nThe results were shocking in their simplicity. Depression was not striking people like random lightning from a cloudless sky. Nearly every severely depressed person had endured an unbearable life blow before the darkness swallowed them. They had lost their homes, suffered abuse, or found themselves trapped in dead-end corners with zero exit routes. Their agony was not an imaginary defect in their biology.\n\nThink of emotional pain like physical pain in your hand. If you rest your palm on a sizzling cast-iron skillet, your nerves scream in agony. You do not curse your nerves for being sensitive. You yank your hand off the hot metal! Deep sadness works the exact same way. It is a brilliant, built-in distress flare telling you that your basic human needs are burning down.",
